<<~ ahu #/head>>

! Epoch Binding Surfaces ~ what a dependency bump actually costs

''The engine CID is not the only surface that binds to an epoch'' (operator, 2026-08-08). Five surfaces
bind, they bind to ''different things'', and only one of them moves when a runtime dependency does.

''This meme measures rather than reasons.'' Every row below reads off the code or a build artifact, because
a wrong answer here reads identically to a right one until a founding breaks.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-five>>

!! The five surfaces, and what each binds TO

<<~ has Surface Seal-epoch "binds/seated-keys + threshold moved-by/a-roster-or-quorum-change ~ genesisSealEpochCid — carries NO dependency, NO blob, NO version">>
<<~ has Surface Hearth-true-name "binds/the-TW5-core-blob-sha256 moved-by/a-TW5-submodule-bump ~ engineCid, and it rides INSIDE every signed delegation preimage">>
<<~ has Surface Plugins-CID "binds/the-built-plugin-blob-shas moved-by/THIS-REPO'S-OWN-SOURCE ~ 23 modules + 115 tiddlers packed from our TS; verified at BUILD time by verifyGenesisArtifact — LOCAL integrity, never a membership criterion, and no peer path reads it (#/the-real-mover)">>
<<~ has Surface On-disk-serde "binds/automerge + keyhive byte formats moved-by/a-RUNTIME-dep-bump ~ the only npm-movable surface">>

!!! ★ ONE MECHANISM — A MONOTONE FRONTIER THAT ONLY ROLLS FORWARD ★

''The word carried three shapes for a while, and two of them were the same mechanism wearing different clothes.'' Named, so nobody re-splits them:

| carrier | what rolls it | why this carrier |
| ''an INTEGER max-register, per-writer slots'' | any writer renewing | the writer-set is FIXED, so a number orders it. `epoch-lease` folds the slots by max; `device-delegation` binds with `boundEpoch` and goes stale when the fold rolls past it |
| ''a HASH-LINKED chain head'' | the kahu quorum, at a seat or rotate ceremony | the writer-set ITSELF rotates, so no number the old set could write would bind the new one. Each link names its parent, so the order is cryptographic rather than numeric (`sealLineage`, head = `sealEpochCid`) |

''Same law, two carriers: a value that only moves forward, and a grant that goes stale when it moves past.'' The chain is the max-register at the charter grain — where what changes is //who may roll it//, which a plain counter cannot express.

!!!! Whose word is it ~ the unblur that has to come first

''Before asking what a shape does, ask who owns the word.'' A word this house mints, it may rule; a word a dependency owns, it MUST NOT redefine — it qualifies at the shore and translates, because redefining an upstream term silently forks the reader's understanding of the upstream's own docs.

| the word | owner | what it names there | our rule |
| ''epoch'' (bare) | ''ours'' | the monotone fencing frontier — `epoch-lease`, bound by `boundEpoch` | RESERVED. Bare //epoch// names this and nothing else (the rule `epoch-handlers` already set) |
| ''seal epoch'' | ''ours'', shape from KERI/TUF | the charter's hash-linked lineage, head = `sealEpochCid` | always QUALIFIED. Same law as the fence, at a grain where the writer-set itself rotates |
| ''engine epoch'' / ''kāhuli epoch'' | ''ours'' | the `hearthTrueName` = `engineCid` roll — a forward-rebind of the mesh onto a new engine content-CID (the kāhuli motion) | always QUALIFIED. It names the PLACE half rolling forward, NEVER the bare //epoch// (the lease frontier); a grep for the fence reads past it |
| ''CGKA epoch'' | ''Keyhive / BeeKEM'', upstream MLS RFC 9420 | a group-key generation; concurrent rolls ''merge by BLANKING'' and hold NO root secret until a later non-concurrent commit | NEVER ours to rule. Keeps its upstream spelling, always qualified. A max-register picks a winner and this does not — carrying our fence onto it costs FORWARD SECRECY (cabal-realm#the-tie-break) |
| ''compaction epoch'' | ''DXOS'', borrowed for `bag-compact` | a snapshot-restart that cuts history behind | always QUALIFIED. It bounds //state-history growth//, never //who may act// — a cut, not a fence |
| ''epoch (ms)'' | ''POSIX'' | a time ORIGIN — 1970, and a wall-clock offset from it | NOT SPENT HERE. Our wall-clock fields read `POSIX ms`, because a reader meeting both spellings in one file has to guess which authority a number carries — and one of them is the global now this house refuses |
| ''L4 Theme / strategic epoch'' | ''ours'' (FfzClock) | a rhythmic BAND name, unbounded | pure homonym. Names no mechanism; a grep for the fence should read past it |

''The test that separates them in one question: does the fold pick a WINNER, or blank both paths?'' Max-register and chain-head both pick; CGKA blanks; compaction picks nothing because it is not a fold at all.

''And a lease rides two instruments, not one'' — the epoch-counter for its VALUE, FfzClock for its CADENCE (ffz-clock#the-two-instruments); how fast the frontier SHOULD roll is paced to the adversary's measured creep, never to convenience (ffz-mesh-timing#the-primary-clock).

''AND THE THIRD SHAPE DIED.'' A vessel's RAISE briefly carried a rolling WINDOW of markers — a moving set, per-Nexus, entered as the Nexus traded and falling off the far end. It refuted on three counts, each fatal alone:
* ''a set answers //is this listed//, never //is this newer//'', so a partitioned island cannot reason about it locally — the one property a causal island needs most.
* ''whatever falls off the far end encodes a retention policy nobody wrote down'', and a count-based lifetime tracks the Nexus's own tempo: a quiet Nexus expires nothing, a busy one expires fast, and the slowest peer becomes everyone's replay surface. In a totally-ordered chain that costs LIVENESS; in a mesh it costs SAFETY.
* ''a list of valid markers IS a roster of moments.'' This house holds no rosters.

A raise is an ordinary capability grant that ends by ''NON-RENEWAL'', which the house already does everywhere else. So `raiseStands` reads `boundEpoch >= effective` against the Nexus's own lease epoch — the same comparison, against the same fold, that every device delegation already makes. ''Read it as: the Nexus is the resource, and its lease epoch is the fence.''

The receiver-side per-peer window (IPsec-shaped, evaluated locally, `undefined` at the edge) stays available for ''anti-replay on a wire'' — packet sequence numbers, where a set is the right instrument. It never governs standing.

!!!! Prior art ~ the field built this discipline, and named the grave

''A monotone forward frontier is a TAGGING DISCIPLINE the deployed field arrives at independently'' — recorded here as evergreen prior-art, never as an advance owed: the engine-advance shape stays `mu`, pending its own recovery.

* ''Veilid `CryptoKind`'' fourcc-tagged keys (VLD0=Ed25519, VLD1=ML-DSA): "every new version is supported alongside the old ones," a separate routing table per cryptosystem, reads auto-select, writes default to newest (veilid.com/how-it-works/cryptography). Version each unit, route by its tag, both coexist.
* ''UCAN'' per-subspec versioned envelope tags (`ucan/dlg@1.0.0`) "enable future protocol evolution without flag-days."
* ''ATProto Lexicon'': backward-compatible changes only (new fields optional, types cannot change), and "if larger breaking changes are necessary, a new Lexicon name must be used," read by fail-open permissive readers — breaking = a NEW object, never a mutated meaning.
* ''Matrix room-version upgrade'': a breaking version spawns a NEW room id, linked by `m.room.tombstone` (`replacement_room`) + the new room's `predecessor`; clients follow the chain forward on their own schedule — a hash-linked lineage as a migration mechanic, the `sealLineage` shape in the wild.
* ''DXOS epochs'': peers cooperatively agree a new epoch that collapses history into a compact baseline without breaking the running stream — the borrowed compaction epoch, at source.
* ''BeeKEM (Keyhive)'' at primary source (inkandswitch.com/keyhive/notebook): concurrent CGKA rolls ''blank-and-merge rather than pick a winner'', and κ trades forward secrecy for concurrent-secret access — exactly the ruling that carrying a max-register fence onto a CGKA epoch costs forward secrecy (`cabal-realm#the-tie-break`).

''Sedimentree confirms the compaction split at source'' (inkandswitch.com/keyhive/notebook). It compacts the Automerge commit graph by recursive content-addressed chunking, with chunk boundaries set by ''the number of trailing zeros in a commit's hash'' so peers with different change-sets agree what goes in each chunk — ''no wall clock, no coordination.'' That answers the test above (does the fold pick a WINNER or blank both paths?): compaction ''picks nothing; it is a cut, not a fold.'' And it states the interaction plainly — because CRDTs need the entire causal history it "sacrifices forward secrecy … but retains secrecy of concurrent and future chunks," and ''member revocation depends on BeeKEM key rotation, NOT compaction'' — verbatim the ruling that a compaction epoch bounds state-history growth, never who-may-act. The trailing-zeros-in-hash rule is the clockless boundary the field supplies for that cut; BeeKEM (κ, blank-and-merge) stays the sole who-may-act authority beneath it.

''ANTI-PATTERN — the killed clock-dependency, running in the wild.'' Willow's prefix-pruning lets a newer short-path entry silently tombstone a whole subtree, and a skewed/hostile clock weaponizes "newer" (willowprotocol.org/specs/data-model) — a live instance of exactly why the rolling-window shape died here (a set answers //is this listed//, never //is this newer//). did:webvh builds the same dependency: it requires "the versionTime of every entry MUST be strictly greater than the immediately preceding entry's" and rejects equal timestamps — a self-asserted-time ordering the signed `handleEventBytes` refuses by carrying NO time field (ordering is pure `seq` + `prevEventCid`). Both confirm "correct even if all timestamps were zero"; no migration marker may resolve on a self-asserted time.

!!!! Where each claimant already stands — the sweep map

''Sited so the next reader corrects nothing that already reads true.'' A sweep that re-derives these will
re-split what this meme collapsed; the whole arc began because a hand-derived model inverted the fact it
meant to capture.

''CANONICAL — cite, never rewrite.'' `convergent-mesh#two-revocation-modes` holds the founding statement
(//the epoch is a LEASE, not a revoker//). `ffz-clock#the-two-instruments` rules value ⊥ cadence, and
`ffz-mesh-timing#the-primary-clock` paces the roll. `cabal-realm#the-tie-break` is the AUTHORITY on the
keying fold and belongs to Keyhive/BeeKEM. `compaction-handlers` reserves the bare word and names compaction.
`FFZ_Chronometer_SPEC_OUTLINE` and `federated-causal-islands#the-edge-island` carry carrier-B at edge grain.

''HOMONYMS — read past them.'' `attention-scale` · `law-of-5s` · `system-pattern-integrities` ·
`ffz-clock`'s L4 band all name a RHYTHMIC //Theme/epoch//, which is no mechanism at all. `causal-islands`
§model-drift names a model VERSION. `sensorium-runbook` forbids the word locally and is a ward, not a gap.

''AND A MARK THAT LIVES ONLY IN A REPORT IS NO MARK.'' This section exists because the first pass named
these sites in conversation and left the tree unmarked — which is the same failure as a green count beside
a check that never ran.

!!! ★ THE ONE MOST EASILY MISSED ★

''`hearthTrueName` IS the genesis `engineCid`, and it rides inside the SIGNED preimage of every
device-delegation edge'':

```
lar-device-delegation/v2|{personaRootDid}|{deviceDid}|{deviceVerifyingKey}|{hearthTrueName}|{issuedAt}|{expiresAt}|{boundEpoch}
```

''So every vessel that ever joined SIGNED the engine CID.'' Move it and every edge names a hearth true-name
that no longer matches — the Binding Gate refuses, and the refusal reads as a fleet-wide eviction nobody
performed. ''The binding IS (vessel × hearthTrueName)'': the engine CID is not a version label, it is the
''PLACE'' half of the two-key atom.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-measurement>>

!! What a runtime dep bump ACTUALLY moves ~ measured 2026-08-08

''The answer reads narrower than the fear, and the narrowness rests on two facts a reader can re-check.''

# ''The engine blob is VENDORED TW5.'' `computeEngineCid` hashes `engine/v1\ncore-sha256:<the TW5 core blob's
  sha256>` — a pure function of those bytes, with the version label deliberately excluded so a re-tag cannot
  manufacture a schism. TW5 rides as a ''workspace submodule'', so ''no npm bump can move it''.
# ''The built plugin carries ZERO automerge references.'' Measured on the packed artifact: 23 modules, 115
  tiddlers, and `grep -c automerge` reads ''0''. The plugin packs THIS REPO'S TS, never the runtime
  dependency tree.

''Therefore'': bumping `@automerge/*`, `@noble/*`, `ws`, `tsx`, `vite` moves ''NO epoch binding''. The seal
lineage stands and every delegation edge still verifies, because the binding rides the engine alone.

''What it CAN break is the on-disk serde'' — stored bytes serialized by a format the new deserializer cannot
read. The vessel already names that condition and its cure rather than boot-looping on it:

> ''Cause'': stored bytes predate a dependency bump (keyhive / automerge / beelay / TW5).
> ''Cure (identity-safe, no data loss)'': run `lares vessel rite rebuild` — rebuilds the genesis engine under current
> deps; your operator key/card are untouched.

''One surface, one cure, and the cure keeps the identity.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-real-mover>>

!! ⚠ CORRECTED — what a source commit actually moves

''AN EARLIER PASS OF THIS MEME CLAIMED THE pluginsCid AS A MEMBERSHIP CRITERION'' — that everyone running
the kahu's ratified `engineCid`/`pluginsCid` PAIR runs the same Nexus, so every commit touching the packed
plugin ''proposes'' a new one. ''Read against the code 2026-08-09, that claim does not stand'', and it went
out at a Synthesis-Canon band on grounds nobody had walked. Recording the correction beside the error,
because a meme that quietly grows a right answer teaches nothing about how the wrong one got in.

''WHAT THE CODE ACTUALLY DOES.'' `pluginsCid` appears in three files, and it gets COMPARED in exactly one
place — `verifyGenesisArtifact`, called from `build-genesis-island.ts`. That runs at BUILD time and asks a
purely local question: does the plugins region recompute to the witness this artifact stored? ''Integrity,
never membership.'' No peer path reads it: the device-delegation preimage carries `hearthTrueName`'s value
and nothing else from the genesis region, and no join, gate or verify compares one vessel's plugins digest
against another's.

''AND THE DOCTRINE SAID SO ALREADY, in the source, in plain words'' (`genesis-artifact#hearthTrueName`):

> The PUBLIC face = the engine content-CID ''ALONE'' — the TW5 core + version, ''NOT the plugins''. Plugins
> compose ''per-operator'' (DreamNet-offered) and ride their own fast ratchet, so a plugin change ''MUST
> NEVER perturb the true-name.''

''So the epoch rides the genesis ENGINE, and beneath it THREE tiers stand named, each on its own
ratchet'' — the standing cut //the engine is universal; the corpus is content// (`bag-wiki-uri-split`):

* ''ENGINE'' (`engineCid` = `hearthTrueName`) — the SLOW ratchet. Signed into every
  device-delegation edge, it BINDS MEMBERSHIP, and it advances by ACT (a TW5-submodule roll, never
  an upgrade — #/the-rule).
* ''GRAMMAR'' (`pluginsCid`, the memetic-wikitext parser) — FROZEN-BY-HASH like the engine, with NO
  edit surface. It advances by ACT too — an operator promotes a new CID — but that act is a
  per-operator CHOICE and NEVER perturbs the true-name; its ratchet binds LOCAL integrity, never
  membership. It runs "faster" than the engine only in binding nobody, never in being editable.
* ''CORPUS'' (the content bags) — the genuinely FAST layer: mutable, live-edited, composed freely.

A commit that re-packs the plugin moves a local integrity witness and touches no lineage: the seal
stands, every delegation edge still verifies, and no confederation shifts. Keeping the three tiers
apart is what lets operators differ without schisming — a grammar each promotes on their own choice,
a corpus each edits live, an engine all share by hash.

''INVARIANT: grammar drift alone does not separate Nexuses — the ENGINE epoch binds who-can-talk.''
Two operators on the SAME engine and DIFFERENT grammars still interoperate, because the grammar
composes per-operator and never touches the `hearthTrueName` each device-delegation edge signed. Only
a DIFFERENT engine reaches the disjoint-station-set break below (no transformation, only translation by
assertion). The engine epoch is the slow ratchet that binds membership; the grammar rejim never
determines who-can-talk.

''THE SHAPE OF THE ERROR, kept as the useful part.'' Two CIDs sat side by side in one manifest, so the meme
read adjacency as conjunction and promoted a local witness to a federation criterion. ''A field's PRESENCE
in a structure never names its ROLE'' — only a reader that follows it to a comparison learns that, and this
one reasoned from the shape instead.

''AND THE OPEN STRETCH STAYS OPEN'' (`genesis-doc#the-open-stretches`): the model version binds at FOUNDING
and ''nothing consults it when two replicas MERGE'' — precisely where the law bites. Two peers materializing
fresh from different engine versions name the ''disjoint-station-set'' break: where two frames share no
co-observed points, ''no transformation exists at all, only translation by assertion''.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-rule>>

!! The rule for a founding

''BUMP BEFORE, NEVER AFTER — and the reason is narrower than it sounds.'' A bump after founding risks the
serde skew alone, which `lares vessel rite rebuild` cures without touching the identity. So the rule reads as ''hygiene
rather than a cliff'': founding on current deps costs one afternoon of testing; founding on stale ones costs
a rebuild at an inconvenient moment.

''The surfaces that would be a cliff — the engine CID and the plugins CID — do not move on npm at all.''
They move when the operator bumps the TW5 submodule or when this repo's source changes, and ''both of those
are acts, never upgrades''.

<<~ has Rule Runtime-deps "when/before-founding cost/a-rebuild-if-skipped surface/serde-only ~ automerge · noble · ws · vite">>
<<~ has Rule TW5-submodule "when/an-ACT-never-an-upgrade cost/every-delegation-edge surface/hearthTrueName ~ moves the PLACE half of the atom">>
<<~ has Rule Our-own-source "when/a-per-operator-ACT cost/LOCAL-integrity-only surface/pluginsCid ~ a grammar advance promotes a new CID and composes per-operator, NEVER perturbs the true-name, NEVER a membership criterion (#/the-real-mover)">>

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/edges>>

